-- Same 8-queen instance as valid-instance.soil, EXCEPT queen2 and queen6's
-- QueenCol links are swapped (queen2 -> col1 instead of col7, queen6 -> col7
-- instead of col1). This is the smallest change the model's own structure
-- allows: row/col distinctness is enforced structurally by the mandatory
-- 1-1 QueenRow/QueenCol bijections (see NQueens.use's header), so a single
-- queen cannot be moved to an already-occupied row or column without USE
-- itself rejecting the !insert as a multiplicity violation at load time --
-- the minimal edit that stays structurally legal is swapping which of two
-- already-used columns two queens point to. This keeps every Row/Col idx
-- value, and the fact that all 8 rows/cols are each still used by exactly
-- one queen, completely untouched (distinctRowIdx and distinctColIdx both
-- still hold), while (as an unavoidable side effect of which two queens
-- were picked) reintroducing exactly one diagonal attack.
--
-- Which pair actually ends up attacking was determined empirically, not
-- guessed: the same independent Python script used for valid-instance.soil
-- exhaustively tried every row-pair column-swap against all 92 known N=8
-- solutions and picked the first swap producing exactly one total
-- attacking pair anywhere on the board. For this solution
-- ([0,4,7,5,2,6,1,3]), swapping rows 2 and 6's columns gives
-- [0,4,1,5,2,6,7,3] -- and the resulting single attacking pair is NOT
-- (queen2,queen6) (the two queens whose links were actually changed) but
-- (queen5,queen6): queen5 sits at (row 5, col 6) and queen6 now sits at
-- (row 6, col 7), so row5-col6 = row6-col7 = -1, i.e. they share the
-- row-idx-minus-col-idx diagonal. (No swap among any of the 92 solutions
-- was found where the swapped pair attacks itself and nothing else --
-- swapping two entries of a valid permutation always risks new collisions
-- with a THIRD, untouched queen, and empirically it always did.) No other
-- pair attacks; distinctRowIdx and distinctColIdx are both still fully
-- satisfied.
--
-- USE checks a class invariant as a single Queen.allInstances->forAll
-- statement, so this single bad diagonal pair is expected to surface as
-- ONE failed invariant (noAttack) overall, not two -- see invalid-instance.cmd
-- for the actual `check -v` output confirming this.

!create row0 : Row
!set row0.idx := 0
!create row1 : Row
!set row1.idx := 1
!create row2 : Row
!set row2.idx := 2
!create row3 : Row
!set row3.idx := 3
!create row4 : Row
!set row4.idx := 4
!create row5 : Row
!set row5.idx := 5
!create row6 : Row
!set row6.idx := 6
!create row7 : Row
!set row7.idx := 7

!create col0 : Col
!set col0.idx := 0
!create col1 : Col
!set col1.idx := 1
!create col2 : Col
!set col2.idx := 2
!create col3 : Col
!set col3.idx := 3
!create col4 : Col
!set col4.idx := 4
!create col5 : Col
!set col5.idx := 5
!create col6 : Col
!set col6.idx := 6
!create col7 : Col
!set col7.idx := 7

!create queen0 : Queen
!create queen1 : Queen
!create queen2 : Queen
!create queen3 : Queen
!create queen4 : Queen
!create queen5 : Queen
!create queen6 : Queen
!create queen7 : Queen

!insert (queen0, row0) into QueenRow
!insert (queen1, row1) into QueenRow
!insert (queen2, row2) into QueenRow
!insert (queen3, row3) into QueenRow
!insert (queen4, row4) into QueenRow
!insert (queen5, row5) into QueenRow
!insert (queen6, row6) into QueenRow
!insert (queen7, row7) into QueenRow

!insert (queen0, col0) into QueenCol
!insert (queen1, col4) into QueenCol
!insert (queen2, col1) into QueenCol
!insert (queen3, col5) into QueenCol
!insert (queen4, col2) into QueenCol
!insert (queen5, col6) into QueenCol
!insert (queen6, col7) into QueenCol
!insert (queen7, col3) into QueenCol
